    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 071738\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n938 AM PST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Gusty north winds linger in the Colorado River Valley today but \n  otherwise quiet weather through at least Thursday. \n\n* Models continue to suggest the return of an active pattern\n  towards the middle of November.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through Friday.\n\nLatest water vapor satellite imagery clearly depicts a closed low \ndiving into northwestern Mexico with a ridge building over the Great \nBasin. The N-S pressure gradient induced at the surface will keeping \nnortherly winds around through the weekend. Gusts will be strongest \nin the lower Colorado River Valley (Laughlin to Needles) today with \ngusts 20-35 mph. As the low weakens and the ridge amplifies over the \ncoming days, winds are forecast to weaken while temperatures rise \ngradually. High temperatures are expected to be 2-6 degrees above \nnormal during this time, resulting in quite pleasant conditions \nacross the region. Dry conditions prevail as precipitation chances \nstay below 5% areawide. \n\nBy the end of the week, ensemble and deterministic guidance (both \nphysics-based and AI) continues to suggest a shift to a more active \npattern for the western US. At this range, specifics are quite \nlimited and uncertain, but there is good agreement on the presence \nof a trough near the Pacific coast. What we receive in terms of \nsensible weather is going to be determined by details such as \nmoisture trajectories and the extent of phasing between the polar \nand subtropical jets, but the overall pattern appears favorable for \nprecipitation chances, cooler temperatures, and increased winds. The \nlatest 6-10 Day outlook from the CPC aligns with this, having 50-70% \nodds of above-normal precipitation between November 12th and 16th. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...\nWinds have shifted to the NNE (010-020 deg) sustained around 10-12 \nkt and are expected to persist through the afternoon hours. An \nisolated gust around 15 kt is possible through sunset. Potential is \nthere for winds to continue to veer more toward the NE (030-050 deg) \nby mid afternoon and toward sunset, but at that time wind speeds \nshould begin diminishing under 10 kt. After sunset, the diminishing \ntrend continues with a directional shift back to the southwest. \nMostly clear skies expected. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Primary wind concerns \nremain in the Colorado River Valley at KIFP and KEED as strong \nnortherly winds 15-25 kt with gusts exceeding  30-35 kt at times \ncontinue through at least sunset. Expect a diminishing trend \ntonight, but still remaining elevated at 10-15 kt with isolated \ngusts. Elsewhere, much quieter conditions expected as lighter \nnortherly flow remains across the region. VFR conditions and clear \nskies will continue areawide.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Woods\nAVIATION...Moore\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
